 WHAT THIS ESTABLISHES

1. The pre-flight refuses correctly against a real device that has no enrolled
   CYVRA sanitization agent. The refusal is reached BEFORE any destructive
   command is issued, which is the ordering standing hold H1 requires.

2. This independently corroborates the 14-Oct PUR-006 baseline run, which
   counted 0 CYVRA packages among 333 installed packages
   (docs/evidence/pur006-product-path-2026-10-14.log). The agent is absent
   because the 12-Oct factory reset removed it, and it has not been
   reinstalled since.

3. The reason text names the route rather than hiding it. Standing hold H8
   requires the weaker route to be described as weaker: purge on this build is
   agent-based, and this device cannot be purged until the agent is installed
   and enrolled as device owner.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT ESTABLISH
 - It does not establish that a purge would succeed on a device that IS
   enrolled. No wipe was issued; issuing one would erase the bench device and
   H1 permits that only as an explicitly commissioned sequence.
 - It does not establish erasure of anything. Erasure is established only by
   post-reset verification (PUR-005), never by the trigger path.

CONSEQUENCE FOR THE NEXT STEP
 Step 5 (purge through the product path with before/after counts) cannot run
 on this device in its current state. It requires, first:
   (a) installing the CYVRA sanitization agent APK, and
   (b) enrolling it as device owner over adb.
 Both are destructive-adjacent setup actions and need the Chief Engineer's
 explicit commissioning.
